---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import data from "../data/tracker.json";
const { labels } = data;
const models = data.models ?? [];
const modelText = models.length ? models.join(" and ") : "a large language model";
---
<Base title="Method · AI Policy Tracker">
  <section class="intro prose">
    <h1>How the tracker works</h1>
    <p class="lede">Everything below runs automatically once a week.</p>

    <h2>1. Which journals</h2>
    <p>The tracker covers every journal graded 3, 4 or 4* in the Chartered ABS Academic Journal Guide (AJG) 2024, across
      all business and management fields. This gives one consistent sample instead of a hand-picked list. Journals can
      also be filtered by two widely used lists: the Financial Times FT50 and the UT Dallas UTD24. Both are re-read every
      week, so their tags stay current. The AJG sample is updated when a new edition appears (every three years). Each
      journal's publisher is checked against Crossref to catch publisher moves.</p>

    <h2>2. Where the rules come from</h2>
    <p>Most journals follow their publisher's AI policy. Some societies and journals add their own rules on top,
      for example on what reviewers may do. Each journal is shown with all the layers that apply to it. Where a
      society or journal rule exists, it takes precedence over the publisher's for that point. Journal-specific rules are
      searched for gradually (about 50 journals a week); until a journal has been checked, its publisher's policy is
      shown.</p>

    <h2>3. Reading the policies</h2>
    <p>Each policy page is downloaded weekly. If its text has changed, a large language model ({modelText}, by
      Anthropic) reads it again and records the points below, with short quotes from the page as evidence. The same
      model searches the web for missing policy pages and reads the FT50 and UTD24 list pages. A change is reported only when one of these points changes, not when a page is merely
      reworded.</p>
    <ul>{Object.entries(labels).map(([, l]) => <li>{l}</li>)}</ul>
    <p>“Not stated” means the page does not address the point; it does not mean the use is allowed.</p>

    <h2>4. Limits</h2>
    <p>Policy pages are written for humans and are often vague, so automatic reading can be wrong. Sources marked
      “verified” have been checked by hand; others were found or read automatically. Always follow the linked
      source page.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
